[01:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so much sense. A good file transfer system needs three pillars. It needs to be fast, secure, and simple. All three at the same time without giving up on any of them. And most tools out there'

[02:04] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: A diagram illustrates the traditional server-based file transfer model (upload then download).
AUDIO: 'pick two and can\'t seem to hit the sweet spot of that Venn diagram. So, let\'s look at each pillar separately. The way most link-based tools work is you upload the file to a server, then the other'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'person downloads it. Sounds fine, but this is actually mathematically slower than it needs to be. If you\'re uploading at 5 megabits per second and downloading at 8 megabits per second, sequential'

[02:29] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: The presenter explains why the traditional upload-then-download model is mathematically slower.
AUDIO: 'upload then download gives you an effective rate of about 3.1 megabits per second. And that\'s slower than either speed on its own. That\'s just what happens when you do things one after the'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'other instead of at the same time. Let\'s talk about secure. A lot of secure transfer tools rely on you setting a single shared password to encrypt the file, which is fine, except passwords'

