AUDIO: 'as secure as its link. SCP is fast and secure, but nobody\'s non-technical friend is going to know how to use SCP right off the bat. So, almost nothing hits all three pillars. But, how is Croc'

[03:43] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: A diagram shows "YOU" connected to "CROC" (server icon) connected to "THEM", illustrating croc's relay model.
AUDIO: 'different? Well, Croc is an open-source Go-based CLI tool built specifically to not compromise on any of these three pillars. So, first of all, fast. Instead of the upload then download model, Croc'

[03:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uses a relay server to set up a full duplex conversation between both computers. Both sides are sending and receiving data at the same time, so you\'re not bottlenecked by the'

[04:09] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Text on screen: "INSTEAD OF A STATIC SHARED PASSWORD" with "password123" in a box, explaining croc's PAKE security.
AUDIO: 'sequential upload download math from earlier. And if both machines happen to be on the same local area network, Croc will actually detect that and transfer locally instead, which is faster still.'

[04:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Next, secure. Instead of a static shared password, Croc uses something called PAKE, password authenticated key exchange. It uses a disposable code phrase, but it\'s not an encryption key'

[04:34] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: A diagram shows two computers connected by a line with an 'X' in the middle, and text "IT ALL FAILS NO DATA MOVES. EVERYONE KNOWS SOMETHING'S WRONG."
AUDIO: 'by itself. Both sides use this phrase in a back-and-forth cryptographic exchange to generate a much stronger key on the fly. If someone tries to intercept that exchange with the wrong phrase, the'

[04:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'whole thing just fails, so no data moves and everyone knows something\'s wrong. And finally, it\'s simple. Single binary, no server to configure, no port forwarding, works straight through NATs'

