AUDIO: 'and firewalls via the relay server, and on the receiving end, you just have to type Croc followed by the code phrase when it asks, and then accept the transfer and it\'s done. But wait, who\'s actually running that relay? Well, by'

[05:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'default, Croc routes your transfer through a public relay run by the tool\'s own creator, Zach himself. Now, because the file itself is encrypted end-to-end via PAKE before it even leaves your machine, that relay only ever sees'

[05:27] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A screenshot of an IBM article titled "Data residency: What is it and why is it important?", discussing data residency concerns.
AUDIO: 'encrypted bytes and connection metadata. It physically can\'t read your file contents, but it\'s still a third-party server sitting in the middle of a transfer by default, and if you\'re at a'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'company with data residency rules or you have a policy against routing traffic through infrastructure you don\'t control, that\'s going to be a blocker. But the good news is that Croc accounts for this. There\'s a built-in Croc relay'

